---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import { conditionMeta, conditionOrder } from "../data/conditionMeta";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
import DryNeedlingLinks from "../components/DryNeedlingLinks.astro";
import FaqList from "../components/FaqList.astro";
import Picture from "../components/Picture.astro";
import { practice, training } from "../data/site";

const title = "Orthopedic Dry Needling in Philadelphia | Five Points Wellness";
const description =
  "Orthopedic dry needling and motor-point therapy for pain and movement with Cara Speer, L.Ac., at Five Points Wellness in Fishtown, Philadelphia.";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", href: "/" },
  { name: "Dry needling", href: "/dry-needling" },
];
const faqs = [
  { q: "Does dry needling hurt?", a: "Most patients tolerate treatment well. Sensations vary and may include a brief twitch, pressure, or dull ache when a trigger point is stimulated." },
  { q: "Is soreness normal after treatment?", a: "Yes. Mild soreness similar to a workout may occur and typically resolves within 24–48 hours." },
  { q: "How many sessions will I need?", a: "Some patients experience relief after one session, while chronic conditions often benefit from a series of treatments." },
  { q: "Is dry needling safe?", a: "When performed with sterile technique by a licensed practitioner, dry needling is generally considered safe and well tolerated." },
  { q: "Can it be combined with other treatments?", a: "Yes. It is often combined with acupuncture, cupping, gua sha, and other supportive therapies for enhanced results." },
];
const extraGraph = [
  {
    "@type": "Service",
    name: "Orthopedic dry needling",
    serviceType: "Dry needling",
    url: `${practice.url}/dry-needling`,
    provider: { "@id": `${practice.url}/#practice` },
    areaServed: { "@type": "City", name: "Philadelphia" },
    description: "Needling of motor points and trigger points for musculoskeletal pain, sports injuries, and movement restrictions.",
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map((item) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumbs={crumbs} extraGraph={extraGraph}>
  <PageHero eyebrow="Acupuncture" lede="Trigger & Motor Point Therapy for Pain and Movement">Orthopedic dry needling in Philadelphia</PageHero>
  <section class="band">
    <article class="narrow prose">
      <p>
        Dry needling is a technique used to treat muscular pain, trigger points, and movement dysfunction using fine, sterile needles. It is based on the same foundational principles and tools used in acupuncture and is considered one application within acupuncture-based treatment approaches.
      </p>
      <p>At Five Points Wellness, dry needling is performed within the broader framework of acupuncture and integrative musculoskeletal care.</p>

      <h2>Clinical Training &amp; Credentials</h2>
      <p>Treatment is provided by a licensed acupuncturist with advanced clinical training including:</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        {training.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>This training provides a comprehensive foundation in both traditional acupuncture theory and modern biomedical approaches to pain and movement dysfunction.</p>

      <h2>How Dry Needling Works</h2>
      <p>Dry needling is commonly used to address areas of tight, overactive muscle tissue known as trigger points. These points may contribute to:</p>
      <ul class="checklist cols">
        <li>Localized pain</li>
        <li>Muscle stiffness</li>
        <li>Reduced range of motion</li>
        <li>Referred pain patterns (pain that travels to other areas of the body)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When a fine needle is inserted into these areas, the muscle may respond with a brief involuntary twitch response. This is thought to help reset abnormal muscle activity, improve circulation, and reduce localized tension.
      </p>
      <p>Many patients report a sensation of release during or shortly after treatment.</p>

      <h2>Trigger Points &amp; Motor Point Therapy</h2>
      <p>
        Many painful muscles contain sensitive areas commonly referred to as "knots" or trigger points. These areas can contribute to pain, stiffness, restricted movement, and even symptoms that travel into other parts of the body.
      </p>
      <p>
        Motor point acupuncture utilizes specific points where a nerve enters and communicates with a muscle. Because these points play a key role in muscle activation, treatment can often help release tension throughout the entire muscle with the stimulation of a single point. This approach is commonly used to address muscle tightness, movement dysfunction, and sports-related injuries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some patients may recognize similarities between this technique and dry needling. While dry needling primarily focuses on trigger points within affected muscles, acupuncture takes a broader approach that may incorporate trigger points, motor points, traditional acupuncture points, and an assessment of the body's overall function. This allows treatment to be tailored not only to the painful area itself but also to factors that may be contributing to the condition.
      </p>

      <h2>What Conditions Can Dry Needling Help With?</h2>
      <p>Dry needling is commonly used for musculoskeletal and movement-related conditions, including:</p>
      <ul class="checklist cols">
        <li>Neck and shoulder pain</li>
        <li>Back pain and postural tension</li>
        <li>Sciatica and nerve-related pain patterns</li>
        <li>Hip, knee, and joint dysfunction</li>
        <li>Sports injuries and overuse conditions</li>
        <li>Muscle tightness and chronic tension</li>
        <li>Headaches and TMJ-related pain</li>
        <li>Restricted mobility or movement imbalance</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What to Expect During Treatment</h2>
      <p>During your session, a detailed assessment is performed to evaluate posture, movement, muscle tension, and areas of pain or restriction.</p>
      <p>A fine sterile needle is then inserted into selected trigger points, motor points, or acupuncture points depending on your condition.</p>
      <p>You may experience:</p>
      <ul class="checklist cols">
        <li>A brief muscle twitch response</li>
        <li>Mild aching or pressure sensation</li>
        <li>A feeling of release in the muscle</li>
        <li>Temporary soreness similar to post-exercise fatigue</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Treatments typically last between 20–60 minutes depending on your needs and whether dry needling is combined with other therapies such as acupuncture, cupping, or manual techniques.</p>

      <h2>How Quickly Will I Notice Results?</h2>
      <p>Many patients report noticeable improvements quickly, sometimes during or immediately after treatment.</p>
      <p>Common short-term effects include:</p>
      <ul class="checklist cols">
        <li>Reduced muscle tension</li>
        <li>Improved range of motion</li>
        <li>Easier movement</li>
        <li>Decreased pain intensity</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In some cases, especially with acute or localized dysfunction, results may be felt right away. Chronic or long-standing conditions often improve progressively over a series of treatments.</p>
    </article>

    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:52rem;margin-top:3.5rem">
      <h2 style="text-align:center;font-size:1.6rem">Range of Motion: <span class="accent">Before &amp; After</span></h2>
      <p class="fine" style="text-align:center;max-width:36rem;margin:0 auto 1.5rem">The images below were taken immediately before and after a single 20-minute dry needling session, demonstrating the improvement in range of motion that can occur within one treatment. Individual results may vary.</p>
      <div class="results">
        <figure>
          <Picture
            name="dry-needling-shoulder-range-of-motion-before"
            width={720}
            height={720}
            alt="Before dry needling at Five Points Wellness, a person in a teal shirt and dark pants stands with both arms lifted out to the sides. One arm sits slightly lower, which is the restricted shoulder position in this range-of-motion pair. The face is cropped out of the frame, and the photo is labeled as the before image."
          />
          <figcaption>Before</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Picture
            name="dry-needling-shoulder-range-of-motion-after"
            width={720}
            height={720}
            alt="After a single dry needling session at Five Points Wellness, the same person in a teal shirt holds both arms closer to shoulder height. The practice published this as the after photo from a 20-minute treatment. The face is cropped out, and the pair is meant to show shoulder range of motion."
          />
          <figcaption>After</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <article class="narrow prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <h2>Dry Needling vs. Acupuncture</h2>
      <p>
        Dry needling is a term commonly used to describe the treatment of muscular trigger points using thin needles. Acupuncture is a broader clinical system that includes musculoskeletal treatment as well as support for nervous system regulation, pain modulation, stress, sleep, digestion, and overall wellness.
      </p>
      <p>At Five Points Wellness, dry needling techniques are integrated within a comprehensive acupuncture-based treatment approach depending on the patient's needs and presentation.</p>

      <FaqList items={faqs} />

      <CtaBand heading="Book a Session" text="If you're experiencing pain, muscle tightness, or movement restriction, dry needling may help restore function and reduce discomfort." />

      <p class="subhead">Dry Needling by Neighborhood</p>
      <p>We're at <strong>2137 E Huntingdon St, Philadelphia, PA 19125</strong>. These pages cover the trip from each area.</p>
      <DryNeedlingLinks except="dry-needling" />

      <p class="subhead">Other Conditions We Treat</p>
      <ul class="chips">
        {conditionOrder.map((item) => (
          <li><a href={`/acupuncture/${item}`}>{conditionMeta[item].label}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
